  "version": "enrolled",
  "operative_text": "(a_in_person_id) not stated.\n(b_no_id_remedy) not stated.\n(c_mail_ballot_id) not stated.\n(d_proof_of_citizenship) not stated.\n(e_other_rule)\n(section) Ohio Const. art. V, sec. 1 (proposed)\n(quote) Section 1. Only a citizen of the United States, of the age of eighteen years, who has been a resident of the state, county, township, or ward, such time as may be provided by law, and has been registered to vote for thirty days, has the qualifications of an elector, and is entitled to vote at all elections. No person who lacks those qualifications shall be permitted to vote at any state or local election held in this state. Any elector who fails to vote in at least one election during any period of four consecutive years shall cease to be an elector unless the elector again registers to vote.\n(other) Ohio Const. art. X, sec. 3 (county charters): adds that county officers and their election are subject to Art. V, sec. 1.\n(other) Ohio Const. art. XVIII, sec. 3 (municipal home rule): adds \"Subject to the requirements of Section 1 of Article V\" before municipal powers.\n(other) Effective date: if approved by voters at the November 8, 2022 general election, takes effect immediately.\n(other) version: 00_IN, 01_RH, 02_PH, 03_RS, 04_AS, 05_EN: all carry the same citizens-only voting text (no ID or proof-of-citizenship text in slots a-d); only cover pages, cosponsor lines and layout differ.\n(other) note: Joint resolution proposing a constitutional amendment for the November 8, 2022 ballot. It says only a United States citizen may vote at any state or local election; it has no ID or proof-of-citizenship rule (slots a-d). The citizens-only language is in slot e.\n(note) Off this topic: the bill sets no ID or citizenship-proof rule.",
